WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.247
A product space records a choice from each of two spaces

00:00:05.247 --> 00:00:12.738
Pair two unit intervals and obtain a square: the first coordinate is chosen independently of the second

00:00:12.738 --> 00:00:18.467
Product topology makes small rectangles the basic neighborhoods in this familiar finite-dimensional example.

00:00:19.767 --> 00:00:23.895
An open rectangle says both coordinate conditions must hold

00:00:23.895 --> 00:00:29.859
Around an interior point, we can independently restrict the first and second coordinates

00:00:29.859 --> 00:00:39.033
More complicated open sets are built as unions of these neighborhoods, connecting coordinate descriptions with the topology of the plane.

00:00:40.333 --> 00:00:43.442
A quotient construction instead identifies selected points

00:00:43.442 --> 00:00:47.440
Send the closed unit interval around the circle once

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:53.214
Its two endpoints land at the same point, while interior inputs remain distinct

00:00:53.214 --> 00:00:58.100
Identifying just those endpoints produces a space homeomorphic to the circle.

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:08.598
The quotient topology specifies which sets count as open after gluing: exactly those whose full preimages are open in the source

00:01:08.598 --> 00:01:14.291
Near the joined endpoint, the preimage includes neighborhoods near both zero and one

00:01:14.291 --> 00:01:18.233
Drawing a loop illustrates this identification, not its proof.
